<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antaeus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antaeus was a giant of Greek mythology, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> and Gaia, who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and forced travellers to wrestle with him, killing them. He drew his strength from contact with the earth, his mother, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> defeated him by lifting him off the ground and crushing him. Ancient writers placed his home and tomb in Mauretania, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> (Tangier) or Lixus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antaeus.jpg" alt="Antaeus" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herakles_Antaios_Louvre_G103.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Antaeus, whose Greek name means opponent, was known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> as Anti and appears in both Berber and Greek mythology. In Greek sources he lived in the interior desert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. According to the Roman geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> his wife was the goddess Tinjis, for whom the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> was said to be named, though it could be the other way round. His daughter Iphinoe consorted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. He was probably incorporated into Greek mythology after the Greek colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> in the mid-seventh century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Antaeus challenged all passers-by to wrestling matches and remained invincible as long as he stayed in contact with his mother, the earth. Because Greek wrestling, like modern wrestling, aimed to force opponents to the ground, he always won and killed his opponents. He built a temple to his father with their skulls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> met him on his way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garden-of-the-hesperides">Garden of the Hesperides</a>, as part of his eleventh labour. Heracles found that he could not beat Antaeus by throwing or pinning him, so he held him aloft and crushed him to death in a bear hug.</p><p>Ancient Greek art shows the myth from the 6th century BCE, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>. Antaeus is bearded and hairy, sometimes shown large and often shrunk for lack of space, and figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> may be present. Scenes where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> lifts Antaeus do not appear at first and may date from the later 5th century BCE, becoming popular in the following century. The contest was a favoured subject of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> sculpture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The location of the home of Antaeus was flexible. When the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> told him that the giant remains of Antaeus lay in a tumulus. His men dug it open and found giant bones, and Sertorius closed it and made offerings. It has been proposed that this monument is the Msoura stone circle, 50 kilometres from Tangier.</p><p>The client king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> claimed descent from a union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and Tinga, the consort of Antaeus. Plutarch recounts a local story in which Heracles had a son, Sophax, by Tinge, who named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> after his mother, and Sophax was the father of Diodorus. Some said Heracles also had a son, Palaemon, by Iphinoe. The ancient city of Barca was also called Antapolis after Antaeus, and Antaeopolis was the Graeco-Roman name of the Egyptian city of Tjebu, whose tutelary god Nemty was identified with him, although that god may be different from the Libyan Antaeus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antaeus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek writers placed the Garden of the Hesperides and the struggle of Heracles with <b>Antaeus</b> nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/lixus">Lixus</a></li></ul></section>
